Dual licensing
==============

This repository contains two kinds of work, each under a different license.

CODE (everything compilable / executable)
-----------------------------------------
Source code, action.yml, configuration, and any executable artifact:
**GNU Affero General Public License v3.0** (AGPL-3.0).

Full text: https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.txt

Code derived from this code is AGPL. Network-deployed forks must publish source.

SKILLS / SPEC PROSE (prose that compiles to behavior)
-----------------------------------------------------
README.md, docs, skill specs, prompt templates, and any prose precise enough
that an agent can compile it to behavior:
**[CC-BY-SA-NS](https://june.kim/cc-by-sa-ns)** — Attribution + ShareAlike + No-Surveillance.

NS (no-surveillance / network clause) closes the SaaS loophole that plain CC-BY-SA leaves open. CC-BY-SA's share-alike clause triggers on *distribution* — giving someone a copy. A company can read CC-BY-SA prose, compile it to code via an agent, serve the code as a SaaS product, and argue they never distributed the derivative. CC-BY-SA-NS treats network use as distribution, the same move AGPL made for code. See https://june.kim/cc-by-sa-ns for the canonical definition.

Commercial use permitted. Attribution required to https://github.com/kimjune01/immune.
Derivative orchestration prose carries the same license — share alike across
forks. The receipts-validation pattern is a commons; specs improve when forks
publish back. See https://june.kim/canon for the canonical argument: prose
that compiles to code is source.

The split exists so commercial maintainers can install the action and run it
on their repos (AGPL on the code permits use; AGPL only kicks in on
*modification* of the code), while spec/prose forks must remain readable
and republishable so the receipts-discipline norm itself stays a commons.
